(message text) "Shows text in the echo area."
(set-mode-line key text) "Shows text in the mode line under key, replacing what key showed before; nil takes it away."
(set-overlay key rows {place :bottom-right scale 1}) "Floats rows over the windows under key, replacing what key showed before; nil or [] takes them away. A row is a line as open-buffer takes it, whose map may give an :opacity from 0 to 1, and a piece [text face :keycap] draws its text as a key. :place is :bottom-right, above the echo area, or :bottom-center, and :scale sizes the text against the editor's."
(set-tab-bar tabs {selected nil on-select nil on-close nil on-new nil on-move nil}) "Shows a bar of tabs labeled tabs across the top of the frame, with the one at index :selected drawn as current; nil takes the bar away. Clicking a tab calls :on-select with its index, its × or a middle click calls :on-close with it, the + calls :on-new, and dragging a tab onto another calls :on-move with both indexes."
(copy-text text) "Copies text to the kill ring and the system clipboard, as M-w does."
(clipboard-set text) "Puts text on the system clipboard without adding it to the kill ring, for secrets."
(notify title body) "Shows a desktop notification with title and body."
(window-focused?) "True while the oxmacs window has the keyboard focus."
(keepass/read path password) "A promise of the entries of the KeePass database at path, each a map of :title, :username, :password, :url, :notes, :totp and :group, leaving out its recycle bin."
(define-command name summary f) "Adds an M-x command called name that calls f. defcommand is the usual way to write one."
(bind-key keys command) "Binds a key sequence such as \"C-c g\" to a command everywhere."
(run-command command) "Runs a command by name, as M-x would, such as (run-command 'load-theme)."
(on-idle key seconds f) "Calls f once each time the editor has had focus and no input for seconds. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(on-open-directory f) "Makes f the way directories open: it is called with the path of each one visited."
(on-open-file extension f) "Makes f the way files ending in .extension open, such as \"epub\": it is called with the path of each one visited instead of reading it as text. A plugin claims the extensions it opens with :opens-files in its plugin.ox, which also wakes it. Registering extension again replaces its handler."
(on-disk-change key f) "Calls f when files the editor shows change on disk. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(on-saved key f) "Calls f with the path of each file the editor saves. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(on-buffer-open key f) "Calls f with each buffer people type in, such as a file, when it opens; plugin buffers don't count. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(on-buffer-close key f) "Calls f with each buffer's handle after it closes, plugin buffers too, so a plugin can let go of what it kept for it. The handle is the one the buffer had while open, but it can no longer be entered. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(on-buffer-change key f) "Calls f after each command or Ox change with a buffer people type in and the edits made to it, a vector of {{start removed inserted}} in character offsets, in order. Changes f makes are reported too. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(on-mode-enter key f) "Calls f with a buffer and the name of the mode it enters, when it opens in one or changes to another. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(on-window-change key f) "Calls f after a command or Ox change that selects another window, shows another buffer in the selected window or changes the layout of the windows, once however many changed together. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(on-diagnostics-change key f) "Calls f when the errors and warnings from language servers, linters and checkers change, once however many changed together. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(diagnostic-counts) "The files with errors or warnings from language servers, linters and checkers, as a map of each path to {{errors: n warnings: n}}."
(on-before-save key f) "Calls f with each buffer about to be saved, and saves it once f returns; an error in f cancels the save with its message, and quitting waits for it. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(on-settings-change key f) "Calls f after the editor's settings change, once however many changed together. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(on-processes-change key f) "Calls f when a process oxmacs runs starts, ends or reports its usage. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(on-evaluate key f) "Calls f with a buffer, a start, an end and whether it failed each time code there runs: an Ox form or file evaluated, the Ox REPL's input, code sent to a console from a buffer that holds it, and zap. Offsets count characters from 0. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(zap start end {error false}) "Tells on-evaluate handlers, such as the zap plugin's, that the code of the current buffer from start to end ran, and with :error true that it failed. Nothing happens while no handler listens."
(annotate start end {face nil note nil underline nil lasts :edit key nil}) "Decorates the characters of the current buffer from start to end, moving with the text around them: shaded in :face, :region by default, underlined with :underline :error or :warning, or with :note, a string shown in :face, :comment by default, after the line where end falls. :lasts :edit keeps it until the buffer next changes, :cleared until clear-annotations, and milliseconds fade it out over that time. Annotating the same characters again with the same :key replaces it."
(clear-annotations {key nil}) "Removes the annotations of the current buffer made with annotate, or only those made with :key."
(show-places name places {title nil header nil ordered false root nil empty nil on-visit nil on-refresh nil}) "Shows places in a list buffer called name, beside the current window. Each place is a map of :file, :line and :column, with an optional :label shown in :face, :text shown instead of the line itself, and :source, the text of code that isn't in a file. Places are grouped by file and sorted, or listed as given with :ordered true; :title heads the list, :header adds lines under it, :root shortens the paths under it and :empty is shown when there are no places. RET and clicks visit a place, or call :on-visit with its map; SPC shows it while staying in the list, n and p step, g calls :on-refresh and q quits."
(on-key-command key f) "Calls f after each key sequence that runs a command, other than typing a character, with a map of :keys, the keys pressed as a vector such as [\"C-x\" \"C-s\"], and :command, the name of the command, and :repeat, true when the same command ran just before. A command run from M-x comes with the keys of M-x. Registering key again replaces its handler."
(processes) "The processes oxmacs runs or ran lately, each a map of :id, :kind, :name, :program, :args, :directory, :env, :status (:starting, :running, :stopping, :exited or :failed), :exit, :pid, :started and :ended in milliseconds since 1970, :buffer, and :cpu percentages and :memory bytes, the last sixty samples while usage is watched."
(stop-process id) "Asks the process with id to end, and ends it with every program it started three seconds later if it hasn't. process/stop does the same with the map process/start gives."
(run-job name program arguments {cwd nil env nil title nil input nil on-line nil on-exit nil}) "Runs program with a vector of arguments in its own buffer named name, shown beside, like M-x compile: its output streams in, g runs it again and C-c C-k stops it. :cwd sets its folder, :env a map of variables, :title the line shown instead of the command, :input text written to its standard input before it is closed, and on-line and on-exit are called with each line and with its exit status. It shows in M-x list-processes, linked to the buffer."
(kill-process id) "Ends the process with id, and every program it started, at once. process/kill does the same with the map process/start gives."
(visit-process id) "Shows the buffer of the process with id beside the current window."
(watch-process-usage on) "Samples the CPU and memory of running processes every second while on is true."
(console-start name {program nil args [] directory nil env {} label nil keys nil on-output nil on-idle nil on-resize nil prompts [] errors [] watch {} prompter nil}) "Starts :program with :args in :directory, with :env variables set, as an interactive console in a buffer called name shown beside, or again in that buffer when its process ended, and returns name. Output marked \\001kind\\002payload\\003 calls :on-output with kind and payload instead of showing, and a prompt marked \\001>\\002prompt\\003 is shown and calls :on-idle once the program waits for input. Unmarked prompts are known by the regex patterns in :prompts, which the unfinished last line must match, and :watch maps kinds to patterns of lines that call :on-output with that kind and the line while still showing. A line matching one of the patterns in :errors after code sent from a buffer reports that code to on-evaluate again as failed. :on-resize is called with the width of its window when it changes. :keys binds keys for every console with that :label. :prompter is a command sent at the start and after each input, for a program such as a shell without a terminal, which prints no prompt of its own: it prints the marked prompt."
(console-send name code) "Sends code to the console called name, showing each line after its prompt as the program reads it."
(console-request name call) "Sends call to the console called name without showing it or its prompt, as plugins ask their program questions; false when it isn't running."
(console-running? name) "True while the console called name has a running process."
(console-waiting? name) "True when the console called name shows a prompt and waits for input."
(console-prompt name) "The prompt the console called name waits at, or nil while it is busy."
(consoles label) "The names of the consoles started with :label label."
(console-history name) "The inputs typed in the console called name, oldest first, as M-p brings them back."
(set-console-history name inputs) "Replaces the inputs M-p and M-n bring back in the console called name, oldest first."
(console-input-start) "Where the input after the prompt starts in the current console, counting characters from 0, or nil outside a console."
(statement-at position) "The statement of the current buffer's language around position as [start end], spanning every line it takes, or nil."
(statement-after position) "Where the first statement after the line of position starts, or nil."
(defun-around position) "The function definition around position as [start end], or nil."
(buffer-language (buffer)) "The language of buffer, a handle or a name, or of the current buffer, such as \"R\", or nil for plain text."
(buffer-list) "The names of every buffer."
(buffer-contents buffer) "The whole text of buffer, a handle or a name, or nil when there is none."
(window-layout) "The windows as data: a buffer name, or [:columns …] or [:rows …] of [weight part] pairs, left to right or top to bottom, as arrange-windows takes them."
(set-word-wrap buffer on) "Makes the long lines of buffer, a handle or a name, wrap between words instead of at the window's edge, when on is true."
(word-wrap? (buffer)) "True when buffer, a handle or a name, or the current buffer, wraps between words."
(set-presentation presentation) "Changes how the windows look from a map: :width centers the text of each window in a column that many characters wide, :hide-chrome true hides the mode lines, line numbers, tab bar and side window, :dim true fades every paragraph but the one with point, :typewriter true keeps point's line in the middle of its window, and :fullscreen true fills the screen. nil puts everything back."
(presentation) "How the windows look, as the map set-presentation takes."
(show-side-window buffer {edge :left width 30 select false}) "Shows buffer, a handle or a name, in the side window, a window of :width columns along the :left or :right :edge of the frame that C-x o and C-x 1 leave alone and C-x 0 there closes; :select true moves to it. Showing another buffer reuses it."
(side-window) "The side window as a map of :buffer, :edge, :width and :selected, or nil when there is none."
(close-side-window) "Closes the side window, moving to the other windows if it was selected."
(select-main-window) "Moves from the side window to the window used last, as C-x o does."
(select-side-window) "Moves to the side window, if there is one."
(store-windows key) "Keeps the windows as they are under key: their layout, buffers, points, scrolling and which is selected, replacing what key kept before. Points move with edits made meanwhile."
(recall-windows key) "Brings back the windows kept under key and forgets them, returning true, or false when nothing is kept there. Windows whose buffers were closed show another buffer."
(forget-windows key) "Forgets the windows kept under key."
(arrange-windows layout {select nil}) "Lays the windows out as layout says: a buffer name, or [:columns …] or [:rows …] of parts, each a layout or a [weight layout] pair whose weights are fractions of the space; parts without one share what is left. :select names the buffer whose window to move to."
(define-language-server name {languages nil program nil args [] programs [] roots [] install "" runs false}) "Lets the editor start the language server called name for files of the modes in :languages, a map of mode names to the language ids the server knows. :program is a name on PATH or a full path, run with :args in the nearest folder holding one of :roots, or else .git; :programs instead lists [program arg ...] vectors, of which the first that is installed runs. :install says how to get it in M-x list-tools, which runs it as a command when :runs is true. Defining name again replaces it."
(language-server-ready?) "True when a language server is ready for the current buffer."
(set-header buffer lines) "Keeps lines, as open-buffer takes them, above the text of buffer, a handle or a name, where they stay while it scrolls, so headings never move the lines below; [] takes them away."
(show-text name text {mode nil read-only false}) "Shows text in the current window in an ordinary buffer called name, made the first time, with point at its start, in the major :mode named, such as \"R\", and :read-only true keeping it from edits."
(prompting?) "True while the minibuffer, a question, a search or a replace is open."
(display-buffer buffer {below nil right-of nil select false}) "Shows buffer, a handle or a name, in a window of its own unless one shows it: below or to the right of the window showing the buffer given as :below or :right-of, or else beside the current window. :select true moves to it."
(complete-at start candidates (explicit)) "Offers candidates in a popup to replace the text from start to point, leaving out the one already typed. When explicit is true a sole candidate is inserted at once and none says so."
(completion-shown?) "True while the completion popup is open."
(remember-location (buffer) (position)) "Remembers the current place, or position in buffer, a handle or a name, so M-, comes back to it after a jump."
(just-typed?) "True when the last command typed a character into the buffer."
(open-file path) "Visits the file at path, returning whether it opened."
(delete-path path) "Deletes the file or folder at path, moving it to the system's trash unless the delete-to-trash setting is off; returns a promise."
(move-path from to) "Moves or renames the file or folder from to to, asking first when to is already there, and points the buffers visiting it at its new place; returns a promise of true once moved, or false when you chose not to replace to."
(move-visited-files from to) "Points the buffers visiting the file from, or files in the folder from, at to, after it was renamed or moved there."
(open-externally path) "Opens a path or URL in the system's own application."
(buffer-file) "The path of the file the current buffer visits, or nil."
(default-directory) "The folder of the current buffer, where file prompts start."
(current-buffer) "The current buffer, as a handle that stays with it when its name changes; with-buffer makes another one current."
(buffer? value) "True when value is a buffer handle."
(buffer-live? buffer) "True when buffer, a handle or a name, is still open."
(enter-buffer buffer) "Makes buffer current for this task until leave-buffer; with-buffer does both around a body."
(leave-buffer) "Ends the last enter-buffer of this task."
(buffer-name) "The name of the current buffer."
(buffer-value key) "What the current buffer keeps under key, a keyword, or nil; with-buffer reads another buffer's."
(set-buffer-value! key value) "Keeps value under key, a keyword, with the current buffer until it closes, replacing what key kept there, and returns value; nil forgets it. A plugin keeps its state for each of its buffers this way, often an atom that the buffer's view reads, and views are built again when it changes."
(buffer-text) "The whole text of the current buffer."
(set-buffer-text text) "Replaces the text of the current buffer with text."
(buffer-exists? buffer) "True when buffer, a handle or a name, is open."
(buffer-shown? buffer) "True when a window shows buffer, a handle or a name."
(buffer-width buffer) "How many columns the window showing buffer, a handle or a name, has, or nil when none does."
(switch-to-buffer buffer) "Shows buffer, a handle or a name, in the current window."
(close-buffer buffer) "Closes buffer, a handle or a name, if it is open."
(current-line) "The line point is on in the current buffer, counting from 0."
(goto-line line (column)) "Moves point to line, and column when given, in the current buffer, counting from 0."
(region-lines) "The first and last lines of the selection as [first last], counting from 0, or nil when nothing is selected."
(deactivate-mark) "Turns off the selection in the current buffer."
(open-buffer name lines {keys nil directory nil file nil watch nil beside false select false on-move nil on-resize nil on-click nil on-drop nil on-type nil on-link nil cursor :block line nil unsaved nil input nil show true}) "Shows lines in a read-only buffer called name, making it the first time, and returns the buffer; :show false makes or fills it without showing it. A line is text, a vector of pieces, or a map of its :line, a :background face and fields of the plugin's own, which make it an item that line-item, goto-item and next-item find. A piece is text, [text face] with a theme face such as :comment or nil, [text face :pill] drawn as a rounded label, or [text face :link target]: follow-link and a click call :on-link with target, and next-link and previous-link move between links. With :file, the buffer shows that file instead: it is named after it as file buffers are, opening the file again comes back to it, and its changes on disk reach on-disk-change. :cursor :line shows point as a highlight across its whole line instead of a block. :keys maps keys to commands, :on-move and :on-resize are called with the line or the width, :on-click with the line clicked and 1, 2 or 3 for a single, double or triple click, :on-drop with the lines a drag started and ended on, and :on-type with each character typed, which then goes nowhere else; buffers that hear clicks or drops don't select text with the mouse. :line moves point there, :beside shows it in another window, which :select true moves to, and :unsaved true marks it %* in the mode line. :input gives it a prompt with an input line below the lines, as in a chat, where M-p and M-n recall earlier input."
(set-lines buffer lines {line nil unsaved nil}) "Replaces the lines of buffer, a handle or the name of a buffer from open-buffer, keeping point or moving it to :line; :unsaved marks it %* in the mode line, or clears that. An input line stays as it was."
(append-lines buffer lines) "Adds lines at the end of buffer, a handle or the name of a buffer from open-buffer, above its input line, keeping windows that were at the bottom there. Added lines can't be undone."
(line-item (line)) "The map the line at point, or line counting from 0, was given as in open-buffer, or nil when it was text or pieces."
(goto-item fields) "Moves point to the start of the first line whose item has every key and value of the map fields, such as {{name: \"a.txt\"}}, and returns the item, or nil when none has."
(next-item (step 1)) "Moves point to the start of the next line with an item, or the one before with -1, and returns the item, or nil when there is none."
(take-input) "The text of the input line of the current buffer, which empties it and remembers it for M-p."
(input-text (name)) "The text of the input line of the buffer called name, or of the current buffer; nil when it has none."
(input-point) "Where point is in the input line of the current buffer, counting from 0, or nil when it is above it."
(set-input name text {point nil}) "Replaces the input line of the buffer called name with text, with point at its end or at :point."
(current-column) "The column of point in the current buffer, counting from 0."
(line-text line) "The text of line in the current buffer, counting from 0, or nil past its end."
(point) "Where point is in the current buffer, as a character offset from 0."
(set-point position) "Moves point in the current buffer to position, stopping at its end."
(buffer-size) "How many characters the current buffer holds."
(insert text) "Inserts text at point in the current buffer and moves point after it; read-only buffers refuse."
(delete start end) "Deletes the text from start up to end in the current buffer and returns it; read-only buffers refuse."
(text-between start end) "The text from start up to end in the current buffer."
(region) "The selection in the current buffer as [start end], or nil when there is none."
(line-start line) "The position where line starts in the current buffer, counting lines from 0."
(line-end line) "The position where line ends in the current buffer, before its newline."
(line-of position) "The line position is on in the current buffer, counting from 0."
(marker position) "A marker at position in the current buffer, which moves with the text around it as the buffer changes."
(marker? value) "True when value is a marker."
(marker-position marker) "Where marker is now, or nil once its buffer is killed."
(move-marker marker position) "Moves marker to position in its own buffer."
(marker-buffer marker) "The buffer marker is in, or nil once it is killed."
(ui/document blocks) "A document for ui/pages, made once from blocks: maps of a :kind, :paragraph, :heading with a :level, :quote, :center, :verse, a line kept as it is, or :item with a :marker and :depth, each with :runs of text or maps of :text with :italic, :bold, :code and :link; :pre with :text; :image with an :image and :alt; or :rule."
(ui/pages document {at [0 0] typeface :serif size 18 spread :auto marks [] header nil on-pages nil on-turn nil on-select nil on-link nil}) "A widget that sets document in pages like a book, showing the page that holds :at, a [block character] place or :end, in a :serif, :sans or :mono :typeface of :size pixels, two pages side by side when wide unless :spread is :single, or always with :double. :marks color [block character] ranges :from :to with a :face, and :header puts text at the top of the left and right pages. :on-pages hears where each page starts and how many pages a turn shows, :on-turn hears 1 or -1 from the mouse wheel, :on-select hears the [block character] ends of text dragged over, and :on-link hears a link's address when it is clicked."
(open-view name render {keys nil beside false select false show true on-resize nil watch nil file nil}) "Shows a view in a buffer called name, built by calling render for ui/ widgets, and built again when it changes, and returns the buffer. With :file, the view shows that file instead of being found by name: it is named after it as file buffers are, opening the file again comes back to it, and its changes on disk reach on-disk-change. :keys maps keys to commands, :beside shows it in another window, which :select true moves to, and :show false makes it without showing it. :on-resize is called with the width and height of the view in pixels when they change, and :watch names a folder whose changes on disk reach on-disk-change while the view is open."
(ui/pdf path {revision 0 zoom :fit-width continuous true dark false marks [] go nil on-view nil on-select nil on-link nil}) "A widget that draws the pages of the PDF at path, once pdf/open has read it, :revision being the one pdf/open gave. :zoom is :fit-width, :fit-page or a scale where 1 is actual size; :continuous false shows one page at a time; :dark true draws pages in the theme's colors. :marks color [left top right bottom] :rect areas of a :page, the :current one more strongly, as pdf/search finds them. :go moves the view once for each new :id: to a :page, showing the point :y down it when given, or by :lines or :screens. :on-view hears the page shown and the scale in percent when they change, :on-select hears the text dragged over, or nil, and :on-link hears the page number or address of a link that is clicked."
(pdf/library (path)) "Offers PDFium in M-x list-tools and returns the PDFium library pdf/open will use: path when that file exists, otherwise the one M-x list-tools installed, or nil."
(pdf/open path) "A promise of the PDF at path, read by PDFium: a map of its :pages count, :title, :author, :outline of maps of :title, :page and :depth, and :revision, which changes when the file does."
(pdf/search path query) "A promise of the places where query is found in the PDF at path, ignoring case unless query has capitals and treating line breaks as spaces: maps of a :page and the :rects, [left top right bottom] in points from the page's top left, that cover the match."
(transient title groups) "Opens a Magit-style menu titled title, of groups that are maps of :title, :switches as [key flag summary] and :actions as [key summary function]."
(prompt question {initial "" choices nil secret false}) "Asks question in the minibuffer and returns a promise of the answer. :initial fills in text, :choices offers names to complete from, and :secret true hides what is typed."
(prompt-path question {directories false initial ""}) "Asks for a path, with completion, and returns a promise of it; with :directories true it asks for a folder. :initial puts a file name after the folder it starts in, as when saving."
(yes-or-no question) "Asks a yes-or-no question and returns a promise of true or false."
(define-mode name title {extensions nil file-names nil comment nil keys nil highlight nil indent nil}) "Defines a major mode called name for files with the :extensions or :file-names given, with its :comment marker, :keys and :highlight function, called with each line's number and text and returning [start end face] spans; one that takes a third argument gets the :state it returned for the line before, when it returns {{spans: [...] state: x}}. :indent, a map of a :width and :after and :dedent patterns, indents a line one width past the nearest line above it when that line matches :after, and one less when the line itself matches :dedent, with tabs when :tabs is true and .editorconfig agrees; without it, RET and TAB keep the indentation of the line above. defmode is the usual way."
(enable-plugin name {config nil disabled false}) "Turns on the plugin called name, with :config for its settings, or turns it off with :disabled true. use-plugin is the usual way."
(plugin-setting plugin key) "The value of a plugin's setting: what :config gave it, or its default."
(configure-plugin name config) "Changes some of the settings of the plugin called name, as use-plugin's :config does, without turning the plugin on; nil puts a setting back to its default."
(settings (changes)) "With a map of settings to values, such as tab-width: 4 and theme: :adwaita-dark, changes those editor settings at once, nil putting one back to its default; without one, the settings in use as a map."
(saved-settings) "The editor settings as custom.ox and init.ox left them, or as last saved by M-x customize, as a map."
(custom-settings) "The editor settings as custom.ox alone set them, before init.ox ran, as a map."
(reload-init) "Runs custom.ox and init.ox again from fresh settings, as saving init.ox does, once the current command finishes."
(plugin-settings) "The settings of every plugin in use, each a map of :plugin, :key, :type, :choices, :doc, :default, :value, :saved and :custom, the last three being what is in use, what the init files left and what custom.ox set; each is nil when the default applies."
(plugin-folder name {cache false}) "The folder where the plugin called name keeps its own files, ~/.oxmacs/data/name, or with :cache true a folder in the system's cache that may be cleared; nil without an oxmacs home. A folder a plugin kept at ~/.oxmacs/name before moves there the first time it is asked for."
(oxmacs-home) "The oxmacs home folder holding init.ox and custom.ox, or nil."
(settings-schema) "Every editor setting as a map of :key, :group, :type, :hint, :unset, :default and :choices, which M-x customize lists."
(ox-reference) "A promise of the reference of every Ox library module and the editor API, as maps of :module and :entries, each entry a map of :name, :kind, :signature and :doc."
(ox-highlight lines) "The lines of Ox code highlighted as Ox buffers show them: for each line, a vector of text and [text face] pieces, as open-buffer takes."
(run-in-repl code) "Shows the Ox REPL beside the current window and evaluates code there, as if it had been typed."
(take-over draw {background nil on-end nil fps nil}) "Takes over the window area until a key or click: each frame, draw is called with a map of :time, :width, :height and :font-size and returns shapes to paint. :on-end is called afterward."
(end-take-over) "Gives the windows back after take-over."
(play-game path {size nil smooth false rate 60}) "Plays the game in the Ox file at path in a window beside the code: the file runs in the user module, start is called once, tick 60 times a second and draw every frame, all looked up by name each time, so redefining them changes the running game. :size is the game's [width height] in pixels, 320 by 180 unless the file calls rodeo/screen."
(pause-game) "Pauses the running game; keys go back to the editor until it resumes."
(resume-game) "Resumes a game that was paused, by hand or by an error that has since been fixed."
(step-game) "Runs one tick of a paused game and draws it."
(stop-game) "Stops the game and closes its window."
(restart-game) "Loads the game's file again and calls start, keeping defonce state."
(game-speed factor) "Runs game time factor times as fast: 0.25 is slow motion, 1 normal."
(game-playing?) "True while a game runs and isn't paused."

(ui/column & children {spacing 0}) "Stacks children from top to bottom, :spacing apart."
(ui/row & children {spacing 0}) "Lays children out from left to right, :spacing apart."
(ui/text value {face nil size nil}) "Text in a theme :face such as :keyword or :comment, at an optional :size."
(ui/button label {on-press nil}) "A button that calls :on-press when pressed."
(ui/space (size)) "Empty space size across, or filling what is left."
(ui/scrollable child) "child in an area that scrolls."
(ui/image image {width nil height nil on-press nil selected false}) "Shows an ox/image image, scaled to fit :width and :height in pixels, or at its own size when the view has room, keeping its proportions. A click calls :on-press, and :selected true frames it in the cursor's color."
(ui/canvas shapes {height nil relative false}) "Draws shapes, :height tall: vectors like [:rect x y width height color], [:circle x y radius color], [:line x1 y1 x2 y2 color width] and [:text x y text color size]. With :relative true, x positions and widths are fractions of the canvas's width, so a drawing fills whatever width it gets."
